WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.767 --> 00:00:01.501
[dramatic music playing]

00:00:02.235 --> 00:00:03.536
It's always exciting.

00:00:03.603 --> 00:00:06.740
Sometimes there is even
between the workmen

00:00:06.806 --> 00:00:08.141
a bit of a competition.

00:00:08.208 --> 00:00:11.878
Who will find first?

00:00:11.945 --> 00:00:14.481
NARRATOR: While conservators
move the painted walls

00:00:14.547 --> 00:00:17.050
to the store rooms
for safekeeping,

00:00:17.117 --> 00:00:21.221
Tarek spots something
in the sand.

00:00:21.287 --> 00:00:23.356
We have a pillar.

00:00:23.423 --> 00:00:28.661
And I can see already there is
a djed pillar decorated on it.

00:00:28.728 --> 00:00:30.797
NARRATOR: It's an
important find.

00:00:30.864 --> 00:00:34.401
This pillar confirms the nature
of ancient Egyptian religious

00:00:34.467 --> 00:00:38.271
beliefs in the years after
the Amarna Revolution

00:00:38.338 --> 00:00:39.906
and Tutankhamun's death.

00:00:42.976 --> 00:00:44.277
This is exciting.

00:00:44.344 --> 00:00:47.180
The deceased is appearing
now, as he would have

00:00:47.247 --> 00:00:49.516
been carrying the djed pillar.

00:00:57.090 --> 00:01:00.827
NARRATOR: The djeds symbol
represents the god Osiris,

00:01:00.894 --> 00:01:07.300
who arose from the dead to live
again as king of the afterlife.

00:01:07.367 --> 00:01:15.408
Ancient Egyptians painted the
symbol on the bottom of coffins

00:01:15.475 --> 00:01:18.845
and wrapped the mummy
with djed amulets

00:01:18.912 --> 00:01:24.818
to summon Osiris and rejuvenate
the soul of the deceased.

00:01:24.884 --> 00:01:29.289
They also carved djed symbols
onto the pillars in their tombs

00:01:29.355 --> 00:01:32.792
to follow Osiris
to the afterlife.

00:01:32.859 --> 00:01:37.230
[mysterious music playing]

00:01:37.297 --> 00:01:40.099
In the time of
Amarna, they told them

00:01:40.166 --> 00:01:43.069
that there is no afterlife.

00:01:43.136 --> 00:01:49.642
After, there was a reaction to
an extreme, worshipping Osiris.

00:01:49.709 --> 00:01:51.544
NARRATOR: The team
has found inscriptions

00:01:51.611 --> 00:01:57.383
to Osiris and Ptah, gods
whose worship Tutankhamun's

00:01:57.450 --> 00:01:59.853
father had forbidden.

00:01:59.919 --> 00:02:02.856
It's more evidence
Tutankhamun had abandoned

00:02:02.922 --> 00:02:05.225
his father's revolution.

00:02:05.291 --> 00:02:07.527
He had restored belief
in the afterlife

00:02:07.594 --> 00:02:11.664
and the power of
all Egypt's gods.

00:02:11.731 --> 00:02:14.734
The religious revolution
demanding the worship

00:02:14.801 --> 00:02:19.239
of a single sun God was over.

00:02:19.305 --> 00:02:22.742
This was a feat that
deserved a magnificent burial

00:02:22.809 --> 00:02:25.879
and a majestic tomb.

00:02:25.945 --> 00:02:29.749
So why didn't Tutankhamun's
mummy get the tomb it deserved?

00:02:35.355 --> 00:02:38.424
In the Valley of
the Kings, Aliyah

00:02:38.491 --> 00:02:41.528
is investigating this mystery.

00:02:41.594 --> 00:02:45.398
Why does Ay banish
Tutankhamun to such a small

00:02:45.465 --> 00:02:47.333
and poorly decorated tomb?

00:02:49.636 --> 00:02:52.839
Aliyah examines Ay's
tomb to look for clues.

00:02:55.375 --> 00:02:56.709
That's the baboon wall.

00:02:59.279 --> 00:03:03.550
Both Tut and Ay opted
for the same scene,

00:03:03.616 --> 00:03:10.456
almost like the same person
chose what goes in each tomb.

00:03:10.523 --> 00:03:12.158
NARRATOR: The
uncanny similarities

00:03:12.225 --> 00:03:15.695
between the two chambers
suggests a common hand

00:03:15.762 --> 00:03:18.131
was at work on both.

00:03:18.197 --> 00:03:22.702
But only Ay's tomb
was fit for a pharaoh.

00:03:22.769 --> 00:03:26.839
It's very similar to the tomb
of Tutankhamun-- the style,

00:03:26.906 --> 00:03:30.009
the artwork, the sarcophagus.

00:03:30.076 --> 00:03:33.746
But it's so much bigger.

00:03:33.813 --> 00:03:36.482
NARRATOR: The artistic
style of the two tombs

00:03:36.549 --> 00:03:39.652
suggests that Ay may have been
responsible for decorating

00:03:39.719 --> 00:03:40.653
both.

00:03:44.757 --> 00:03:48.595
Investigators now suspect
that when Tutankhamun died

00:03:48.661 --> 00:03:52.932
unexpectedly young, the lavish
tomb he ordered for himself

00:03:52.999 --> 00:03:55.969
was not finished.

00:03:56.035 --> 00:03:57.704
Ay seized the moment.

00:03:57.770 --> 00:04:00.873
He ordered Tut be buried
in a smaller tomb.

00:04:00.940 --> 00:04:04.143
It was quickly decorated
and sealed before the paint

00:04:04.210 --> 00:04:06.512
had a chance to dry.

00:04:06.579 --> 00:04:10.049
With Tutankhamun gone, and
before any challengers could

00:04:10.116 --> 00:04:13.753
oppose him, Ay crowned
himself pharaoh

00:04:13.820 --> 00:04:16.422
and decreed that
when he died, he

00:04:16.489 --> 00:04:18.625
would take Tutankhamun's tomb.

00:04:21.961 --> 00:04:23.496
[dramatic music playing]

00:04:24.597 --> 00:04:27.533
Ay buried Tutankhamun
in the smaller tomb,

00:04:27.600 --> 00:04:32.839
so he could have the
bigger tomb for himself.

00:04:32.905 --> 00:04:34.540
This is the tomb
that was intended

00:04:34.607 --> 00:04:40.513
for Tutankhamun, the tomb of Ay

00:04:40.580 --> 00:04:44.050
NARRATOR: Ay banished
Tutankhamun to an unworthy tomb

00:04:44.117 --> 00:04:48.321
to secure his place as pharaoh.

00:04:48.388 --> 00:04:50.723
Later pharaohs
erased Tutankhamun

00:04:50.790 --> 00:04:56.829
from history, smearing his
name as the son of a heretic.

00:04:56.896 --> 00:05:00.933
But 100 years ago, when
his tomb was discovered,

00:05:01.000 --> 00:05:05.605
Tutankhamun was
reborn a superstar.

00:05:05.672 --> 00:05:12.879
Now experts use the clues to
piece together his true legacy

00:05:12.945 --> 00:05:16.315
as a boy king dealing with
the aftermath of his father's

00:05:16.382 --> 00:05:18.084
religious revolution.

00:05:20.653 --> 00:05:23.690
[music playing]

